          <p>
            A power station's weight scales with its battery, and LFP cells, though safer and longer-lived than the older NMC chemistry, store less energy per pound. That is why even the lightest 1kWh units weigh more than 20 pounds. But weight does not scale perfectly. Small units carry a penalty for their case, inverter, screen and ports, which is why the 286 to 288Wh units sit around 29 to 32Wh per pound while the Explorer 2000 v2 reaches about 51. Two River 3 Plus units would weigh about 20 pounds for 572Wh, while one Explorer 1000 v2 gives you nearly twice that energy in a slightly heavier single box. If your daily budget clearly calls for 1kWh, buy one 1kWh unit rather than stacking small ones.
          </p>

          <p>
            The calculation flips when you have to carry it. A walk-in tent site, a beach a quarter mile from the lot, or a canoe portage changes the value of every pound. At those sites a 10-pound unit plus a 6 to 10-pound folding panel beats a 24-pound battery, because the panel keeps producing every sunny day while the battery only empties. For truly ultralight trips such as backpacking, bikepacking and paddling, skip power stations altogether and carry a pocket power bank; our <a href="/best-portable-chargers-for-power-outages/">portable chargers guide</a> covers banks that weigh a pound or less. Flying rules are strict as well: lithium batteries above 160Wh are generally not allowed on passenger aircraft, so every unit here travels by road.
          </p>
