          <p>
            Three items dominate most budgets. The cooler is the big one, because it runs around the clock; on a hot day in the sun with the lid opening every half hour it can approach the top of that range, and in a shaded spot at night it may barely run. The CPAP is next, and the heated humidifier is the deciding factor: with it switched on, nightly use can climb to several times the dry figure, so most campers switch it off or use a travel setting without heat. The third is anything you are tempted to bring from home that heats water or air. A kettle, a hair dryer and a space heater can each use more energy in fifteen minutes than your phones use in a week.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">From List to Battery Size</h3>

          <p>
            Add up the daily figures, then adjust for how each device is powered. Loads on the AC outlets lose roughly 15 percent to the inverter, so use the familiar rule, usable energy equals capacity in watt-hours times 0.85, and divide by the load to estimate runtime. Loads on DC outputs lose much less. Multiply the daily total by the number of days you will go without recharging, then subtract whatever you can reliably put back from driving and solar. What is left is the capacity to buy, plus a margin of 20 percent or so for cloudy days and the odd extra charge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be honest about the extras. E-bike batteries are the classic budget-breaker: a typical pack holds 400 to 750Wh, and charging it through the inverter and the bike's own charger loses energy at both steps, so one full charge can empty a 1kWh unit that was meant to run the cooler. Drone batteries are similar on a smaller scale. If a load only happens once a trip, plan to do it at a campground hookup or while driving rather than sizing the whole battery around it.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">QUICK SANITY CHECK</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Three budgets, three answers.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If your daily budget is under 200Wh, a 300Wh unit lasts a weekend with no recharging. If it is 300 to 700Wh because of a cooler, a 300Wh unit needs daily solar or driving to keep up, and a 1kWh unit gives you one to three days of slack. If it is over 1,000Wh, you are in RV territory and should look at 2kWh with solar.</p>
          </div>
