                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Air conditioning</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Short bursts at most</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, with a large enough unit</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Multi-week trips without sun</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Struggles</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Runs as long as fuel lasts</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            That is also why a growing number of RV owners use a battery overnight and run a generator, where allowed, only during the permitted daytime window to recharge it. The hybrid gets the best of both: fuel-based energy for the long haul and silent power for the hours when it matters. If you already own a generator, a power station turns it from an all-night nuisance into a two-hour morning chore.
          </p>

          <p>
            The generator still wins in two cases. Rooftop air conditioning draws far more than any portable battery can sustain, and a multi-week trip through cloudy country without hookups will outlast any solar setup a car can carry. For everything else, the battery is the better campsite neighbor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weather" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Cold Nights and Hot Cars
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cold</h3>

          <p>
            Lithium batteries should not be charged below freezing, and most power stations block charging at around 32 degrees F (0 degrees C) to protect the cells. Discharging in the cold is allowed within the manufacturer's range, but usable capacity falls as temperature drops, so a unit that lasts two days in July may last noticeably less on a frosty October trip. In shoulder season, keep the power station inside the tent or the vehicle cabin overnight, off the cold ground, and insulate it with a jacket or sleeping bag stuff sack, with the vents clear. If a unit has been sitting in a freezing truck bed, let it warm up before plugging in a solar panel.
          </p>

          <p>
            In cold weather you will also be tempted by electric blankets and heaters. A 12V heated blanket at modest settings is manageable on a 1kWh unit, but a 1,500W space heater will drain even the Explorer 2000 v2 in little more than an hour. Warm clothing, a good sleeping bag and a hot water bottle filled from the camp stove remain far better value per watt-hour than anything electric.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heat</h3>

          <p>
            Summer is the bigger risk for most campers. A closed car parked in the sun gets far hotter than the outside air, easily beyond what any battery maker allows. Many units list an upper operating limit somewhere around 104 to 113 degrees F (40 to 45 degrees C); check your manual. High heat shortens battery life and can trigger shutdowns just when the cooler needs power most. Keep the station in shade, out of the trunk on hot days, and never charge it in direct sun; run a long cable to the panel instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coolers work harder in heat too, which is the other reason a summer trip needs a bigger budget than a spring one. A cooler in a sunny spot on a 95-degree afternoon can run close to continuously, pushing daily use to the top of the range in the load table. Shade it, keep it full, and open the lid less often, and the same battery lasts noticeably longer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rain and Dust</h3>

          <p>
            None of these units is waterproof. Keep them off the ground, under a vestibule or awning, and away from condensation dripping off tent walls. A dry bag or plastic bin with the lid propped for ventilation works in a downpour. Dust is the enemy of cooling fans in desert camping; wipe the vents and keep the unit off sandy ground, ideally on a small folding table or the tailgate.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="trip-examples" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Three Worked Trip Examples
          </h2>

          <p>
            These examples use the daily figures from the load table and the runtime rule above. Your own numbers will differ, and the point is the method rather than the exact totals. Each example starts from the loads, then works out what the battery and the recharging plan have to supply.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">1. The Weekend Tent Trip (Two Nights, Two People)</h3>

          <p>
            Version A, no electric cooler: two phones charged daily (40Wh), string lights for four hours at 10W (40Wh), headlamps and a camera (30Wh), and a Bluetooth speaker (20Wh). That is about 130Wh a day, almost all on USB, or roughly 260Wh for the weekend. A River 3 Plus or SOLIX C300X does it with no recharging and a little left over for the drive home.
          </p>

          <p>
            Version B, with a 12V cooler: add a cooler at around 400Wh a day on the 12V socket, and the daily total becomes about 530Wh, or over 1,000Wh for the weekend. Now a 300Wh unit lasts barely half a day on its own. There are three ways to make it work: a 1kWh unit such as the Explorer 1000 v2 covers the full weekend with no charging at all; a River 3 Plus plus a 200W panel in good sun nearly keeps pace day to day; or pre-chill the cooler at home and use block ice to cut its run time. For most car campers who bring a cooler, the 1kWh unit is the stress-free choice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">2. The Week-Long Overland Trip (Seven Days, Two People)</h3>

          <p>
            The daily budget: a 12V fridge running full time (about 500Wh), lights (50Wh), phones and a GPS unit (80Wh), a laptop for photos and maps (60Wh), and three drone batteries charged from a car charger (about 175Wh with losses, depending on the packs). Call it roughly 865Wh a day, or about 6kWh over the week, far more than any portable unit carries.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the trip runs on recharging. Three hours of daily driving on the 12V socket returns about 300Wh. A 200W folding panel at camp adds roughly 500 to 700Wh on a clear day. Together that is around 800 to 1,000Wh a day, roughly break-even with consumption. A 1kWh battery is then the buffer, not the fuel tank: it covers one cloudy day or a rest day without driving. That is why the Delta 3 Plus fits so well. Start with the base unit, add an extra battery for more cloudy-day insurance, or fit an alternator charger so driving days fully replenish the battery. The Explorer 2000 v2 works too, with a two-day buffer instead of one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">3. RV Boondocking (Four Nights, No Hookups)</h3>

          <p>
            A couple in a small travel trailer, with the fridge running on propane: CPAP on DC for eight hours (320Wh), a roof vent fan at 30W for eight hours (240Wh), LED lights (100Wh), a TV or laptop for three hours at 60W on AC (about 210Wh after inverter losses), a coffee maker for ten minutes at 1,000W (about 200Wh with losses), and phones and small devices (60Wh). The total is about 1,130Wh a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            An Explorer 2000 v2 provides about 1,700Wh of usable AC energy at the 0.85 rule, somewhat more for the DC loads, so it covers about a day and a half alone. With 400W of portable solar returning around 1,000 to 1,400Wh on a sunny day, the system roughly breaks even and the four nights work, with the trailer's own house battery handling the water pump and furnace fan. If the forecast is cloudy, swap the coffee maker for a stovetop percolator and the budget drops by almost a fifth. What this setup will not do is run the rooftop air conditioner, which commonly draws 1,300 to 2,000W while running; that remains a job for shore power or a generator.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rv-van" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. RV and Van Use
          </h2>

          <p>
            A portable power station can do a lot in an RV or camper van, but it helps to understand where it fits alongside the vehicle's own electrical system. Most rigs already have a 12V house battery, a converter that charges it from shore power, and a shore-power inlet. A power station can sit beside that system or feed into it, and the two approaches behave very differently.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a standalone power source, the station goes on the floor or a shelf and devices plug directly into it. This works in any rig, needs no wiring, and moves with you to the picnic table or the house. Most van-lifers and small-trailer owners start here, and many never need more. It is also the approach that lets the same unit cover outages at home between trips.
          </p>

          <p>
            Feeding the RV's shore-power inlet is the other route. Some larger stations include an RV-style 30A outlet, and adapters exist for plugging the RV's shore cord into a power station. This powers the RV's 120V outlets, but the rig's converter will also try to charge the house battery from the station, wasting energy, and anything big on the AC circuits, such as the air conditioner, the electric water heater element or an electric fridge mode, can overload the inverter instantly. If you go this route, switch off those loads and the converter at the breaker before connecting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mounting and ventilation matter on the move. Secure the unit so it cannot slide or tip under braking, keep its vents clear of bedding and gear, and keep it out of the hottest corner of the vehicle. LFP chemistry is among the most stable lithium types, but any large battery deserves a secured, ventilated spot away from where you sleep with your head against it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Size honestly. A 1kWh unit is fine for a van with a 12V fridge, lights and devices. Once a CPAP, a TV and a coffee maker are added, a 2kWh unit and a few hundred watts of solar become the realistic minimum. Rooftop air conditioning, electric heat and electric cooking at length are beyond any portable station on this list. If you want those, a built-in lithium house battery with a large inverter, or a quiet generator, is the right tool. Owners who want one battery to serve both the rig and the house should read our <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">home backup power station guide</a>, because that job tends to push the size up.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which One to Pack
          </h2>

          <p>
            For phones, headlamps, cameras and lights for a weekend on a budget, the Anker SOLIX C300X is the pick. It is the camping power bank that charges a whole group's devices over USB-C for several days, and at about 9 pounds it can live in the car all season.
          </p>

          <p>
            For tent camping and day trips with lights, devices and a CPAP, take the EcoFlow River 3 Plus. Add a 100 to 200W panel or the extra battery if you also want to run a small cooler. For drive-in car camping with a 12V cooler for two to four nights, the Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 is the lightest 1kWh unit here, and the Bluetti Elite 100 V2 is the alternative when it is the better price or the trunk is tighter.
          </p>

          <p>
            For overlanding and week-long vehicle trips, choose the EcoFlow Delta 3 Plus, paired with solar and ideally an alternator charger, with the option to add a battery later. For RVs, vans and boondocking with a CPAP, fans and a coffee maker, the Jackery Explorer 2000 v2 with 400W or more of solar is the realistic minimum.
          </p>

          <p>
            Torn between the two biggest brands? Our <a href="/ecoflow-vs-jackery/">EcoFlow vs. Jackery breakdown</a> compares the ecosystems head to head, including how the Explorer 1000 v2 and the Delta 3 Plus differ on expansion and charging. And if the same unit also needs to cover outages at home, remember that home backup pushes the size up while camping pushes it down; the <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station rankings</a> show where each capacity class lands.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you choose, the habits matter as much as the hardware. Run what you can on DC, keep the inverter off when nothing needs it, keep the battery out of the sun and off the frozen ground, and plan the recharge before the trip rather than at the campsite. A modest battery used that way outlasts a big one used carelessly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by Capacity Instead of a Load Budget</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most campers buy twice the battery they need and half the charging. Add up what each device actually uses per day, multiply by the days without recharging, and subtract what driving and solar return. That number, plus a 20 percent margin, is the size to buy, not the biggest box that fits the budget.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the Cooler and CPAP Through the Inverter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plugging a 12V cooler's AC adapter or a CPAP's standard brick into the AC outlets throws away 10 to 15 percent of the energy as inverter heat. Use the 12V car-style socket for the cooler and the maker's DC cord for the CPAP, and keep the AC button off unless something needs it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Charging From the Car Socket With the Engine Off</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A power station happily drains a parked vehicle's starter battery through the 12V socket. Leave it connected overnight and you can wake up unable to start the car at a remote trailhead. Only charge from the socket while driving, and use an alternator charger that cuts out when the engine stops.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the Battery in a Hot Car or Frozen Truck Bed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A closed car in summer sun gets hotter than most units' operating limits, and most units block charging below freezing. Heat shortens battery life and triggers shutdowns, while cold cuts runtime. Keep the station shaded by day and inside the tent or cabin overnight, with its vents clear.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting an Untested CPAP Setup</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Assuming the CPAP will run all night on a new power station is a gamble with your sleep. Confirm the correct DC adapter with the CPAP maker, switch off the heated humidifier unless the battery is large, and run the full setup at home for a complete night before relying on it at a remote site.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size portable power station do I need for camping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Size it from a daily load budget. Add up what each device uses per day in watt-hours, multiply by the days without recharging, and subtract what driving and solar can return. Phones, lights and cameras alone need 100 to 200Wh a day, which a 300Wh unit covers for a weekend. A 12V cooler raises that to roughly 400 to 800Wh a day, pointing to a 1kWh unit. RV trips with a CPAP, fans and a coffee maker suit a 2kWh unit with solar.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a power station run a 12V camping cooler?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 12V compressor cooler draws roughly 40 to 60W while running but cycles on and off, so daily use is typically 300 to 700Wh depending on size, heat, sun and lid openings. From the 12V socket, a 286 to 288Wh unit lasts between half a day and a day, a 1kWh unit roughly two to three days, and a 2kWh unit four days or more. Shade, pre-chilling and a full load all stretch runtime.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a CPAP from a power station while camping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is one of the most common reasons campers buy one. A CPAP without its heated humidifier typically draws 30 to 60W, or about 250 to 500Wh over an eight-hour night. The maker's 12V DC cord avoids inverter losses. A 300Wh class unit usually covers one night, a 1kWh unit several. The heated humidifier can multiply use several times over, so test the full setup at home first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a power station better than a portable generator for camping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most campers, yes. A battery is silent, produces no exhaust, can be used inside a tent or RV, needs no fuel and is allowed in loops that ban generators or restrict them to set hours. A gas inverter generator still wins for rooftop RV air conditioning or weeks off-grid without reliable sun. Many RV owners combine the two, running the generator in permitted hours to recharge the battery.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I charge a power station from my car while driving?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Every unit in this guide accepts charging from a vehicle's 12V accessory socket. Most sockets are fused at 10 to 15 amps, so charging is limited to roughly 100 to 120W, or about 100Wh per hour of driving. That refills a 300Wh unit in around three hours; a 1kWh unit needs about ten. Dedicated alternator chargers deliver several hundred watts while the engine runs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much solar do I need for camping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A portable panel produces roughly 60 to 75 percent of its rating in good midday sun, and a clear day offers four to five peak sun hours. A 100W panel returns about 250 to 350Wh a day, 200W about 500 to 700Wh, and 400W around 1,000 to 1,400Wh. Use 100W for devices, 200W to sustain a cooler, and 400W or more for overland fridges or RV use. Keep a buffer for shade and cloud.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a camping power station charge an e-bike?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, if the e-bike charger's wattage is within the station's AC output. Many e-bike packs hold roughly 400 to 750Wh, and charging through the inverter and the bike's brick loses energy at both steps. A 300Wh unit adds only a partial charge, and a 1kWh unit can fill one mid-size pack but leaves little for the cooler. For regular e-bike charging, use a 2kWh unit with solar or a campground hookup.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

