        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Camping power is a budget, not a catalog choice. Write down the daily loads, put the cooler and CPAP on DC, and work out what driving and solar can return before you pick a capacity. For most tent campers that lands on a roughly 300Wh unit like the River 3 Plus; a cooler for several days pushes it to 1kWh, and an RV with a CPAP and coffee maker to 2kWh plus solar.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Keep the battery shaded, out of freezing truck beds and off the car socket with the engine off, and it will outlast many seasons of trips. When you need air conditioning or weeks off-grid without sun, a generator is still the right tool; for everything else, the silent battery is the better campsite neighbor.
          </p>
